1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:08,868
Explosive fire. Bursts of color and light. Unique artworks. Joyous celebrations.

2
00:00:08,869 --> 00:00:18,180
Their language is universal. Their appeal timeless. Fireworks. Now on Modern Moders.

3
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:41,260
They make the old feel young again, and the young feel glorious.

4
00:00:42,020 --> 00:00:44,620
Even the simplest sparklers demand attention.

5
00:00:45,420 --> 00:00:48,980
Aerial spectaculars hold us spellbound with their power and beauty.

6
00:00:49,360 --> 00:00:54,560
To the Japanese, they are hanabe, flowers of fire.

7
00:00:54,561 --> 00:01:04,020
There's something magical that goes back to our basic roots as human beings and the mystery of fire.

8
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:09,840
To see the sky explode from darkness into this bright light and feel these concussions.

9
00:01:10,220 --> 00:01:15,320
It gets somewhere right in the center of our brain and says, now this is something special.

10
00:01:16,540 --> 00:01:23,880
An ancient epigram says that he who has smelled the smoke can never again escape it.

11
00:01:24,560 --> 00:01:29,640
And for many enthusiasts, their fascination begins in childhood.

12
00:01:36,140 --> 00:01:40,080
The fun of fireworks when you were a kid was the first time you were an alchemist.

13
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:43,660
You could touch something to a fuse and a rocket would go up in the air.

14
00:01:43,960 --> 00:01:47,300
The sense of power almost, you could send a thing soaring into the heaven

15
00:01:47,301 --> 00:01:50,620
or scare the cat or scare your parents in particular.

16
00:01:50,621 --> 00:01:57,620
Whatever their age, fireworks craftsmen delight in thrilling crowds.

17
00:01:57,621 --> 00:02:05,620
They are constantly striving to create bigger shows, more dazzling effects, and appreciative audiences.

18
00:02:05,621 --> 00:02:15,620
Early each spring, two weeks of festivities precede the most famous horse race in America, the Kentucky Derby.

19
00:02:15,621 --> 00:02:22,900
half a million fans flock to Louisville, Kentucky

20
00:02:22,901 --> 00:02:25,320
to indulge their love of horses

21
00:02:25,321 --> 00:02:29,380
and to experience one of the largest firework shows

22
00:02:29,381 --> 00:02:30,480
in the world

23
00:02:30,481 --> 00:02:32,780
thunder over Louisville

24
00:02:32,781 --> 00:02:38,860
24 hours before showtime

25
00:02:38,861 --> 00:02:42,860
hearty early arrivals stake out the best vantage points

26
00:02:42,861 --> 00:02:44,800
on the banks of the Ohio River

27
00:02:44,801 --> 00:02:47,640
30 floors above the ground

28
00:02:47,641 --> 00:02:49,140
in Command Central

29
00:02:49,141 --> 00:02:51,380
show producer Wayne Hettinger

30
00:02:51,381 --> 00:02:53,820
oversees the final countdown to Thunder

31
00:02:53,821 --> 00:02:57,600
we're always tasked with kicking off

32
00:02:57,601 --> 00:02:59,880
a bigger event for the Kentucky Derby Festival

33
00:02:59,881 --> 00:03:01,680
that's my job to make sure that it's got

34
00:03:01,681 --> 00:03:03,080
a bigger and better look to it

35
00:03:03,081 --> 00:03:05,660
there's no doubt in my mind we're going to lay it

36
00:03:05,661 --> 00:03:06,880
on the line good this year

37
00:03:06,881 --> 00:03:11,640
Thunder over Louisville is the creation

38
00:03:11,641 --> 00:03:13,480
of the famed Zambelli family

39
00:03:13,481 --> 00:03:15,120
of Newcastle, Pennsylvania.

40
00:03:15,940 --> 00:03:19,400
For the past century, three generations of Zambellis

41
00:03:19,401 --> 00:03:21,920
have brightened skies and lifted spirits

42
00:03:21,921 --> 00:03:24,000
at events around the world.

43
00:03:25,140 --> 00:03:28,580
Presidential inaugurations, royal salutes,

44
00:03:29,520 --> 00:03:31,140
the American Bicentennial.

45
00:03:32,420 --> 00:03:35,120
These and scores of other memorable occasions

46
00:03:35,121 --> 00:03:37,940
have felt the Zambelli's special touch.

47
00:03:40,460 --> 00:03:43,100
For the national victory celebration

48
00:03:43,101 --> 00:03:44,940
after Operation Desert Storm,

49
00:03:44,941 --> 00:03:46,860
they welcome the troops home

50
00:03:46,861 --> 00:03:49,500
with a specially designed yellow ribbon pattern

51
00:03:49,501 --> 00:03:51,440
in the skies over Washington.

52
00:03:55,720 --> 00:03:57,940
These and other patterns are produced

53
00:03:57,941 --> 00:04:00,500
by laying chemical pellets in a shell

54
00:04:00,501 --> 00:04:03,760
along a cardboard cutout of the desired shape.

55
00:04:05,720 --> 00:04:08,280
Filler material surrounds the chemical pellets,

56
00:04:08,600 --> 00:04:09,740
also known as stars.

57
00:04:09,741 --> 00:04:15,980
Once airborne, a fuse ignites the stars and they burst out to form the pattern.

58
00:04:19,980 --> 00:04:24,400
George Zambelli Sr. has directed the company since 1957

59
00:04:24,401 --> 00:04:28,600
and now proudly goes by the nickname of Mr. Fireworks.

60
00:04:29,240 --> 00:04:34,780
For the Thunder extravaganza, he oversees scores of highly skilled pyrotechnicians.

61
00:04:34,781 --> 00:04:41,020
My father would often say it takes longer to be a good paratechnician than to be an

62
00:04:41,021 --> 00:04:42,020
MD.

63
00:04:42,021 --> 00:04:43,560
So we all know how long that takes.

64
00:04:43,561 --> 00:04:45,840
It just takes time because it's not in a book.

65
00:04:45,841 --> 00:04:49,300
You've got to learn the process, you've got to learn the mixtures, the formulas, you've

66
00:04:49,301 --> 00:04:52,080
got to try them out, you've got to experiment with them.

67
00:04:52,081 --> 00:04:55,080
It's a long process.

68
00:04:55,081 --> 00:04:56,504
George Zambelli Jr.

69
00:04:56,505 --> 00:05:01,900
is an MD, an ophthalmologist, who remains heavily involved in the family

70
00:05:01,901 --> 00:05:02,900
business.

71
00:05:02,901 --> 00:05:07,540
There are 24 power technicians working on the bridge today.

72
00:05:07,541 --> 00:05:12,800
They're not only putting in the final mortars, but they're also checking all the connections,

73
00:05:13,560 --> 00:05:19,180
putting in the field modules, which will connect to the main module, and then to the laptop computer.

74
00:05:20,880 --> 00:05:27,680
This year's show contains an astonishing 40,000 shells, weighing over 50 tons.

75
00:05:27,681 --> 00:05:34,680
Every single shell is handmade and each one must be carefully mounted in its own mortar.

76
00:05:34,681 --> 00:05:41,680
They are designed with a specific pattern and use chemicals that burn with the prescribed color.

77
00:05:42,680 --> 00:05:50,680
Well, we really wouldn't want to lean over one of the tubes in case static electricity would fire one of the rockets.

78
00:05:50,681 --> 00:05:55,680
But standing here if one of the rockets fired, we'd be relatively safe.

79
00:05:57,680 --> 00:06:04,720
Back in the command center, the production team keeps careful watch over the mainframe computer designed to run the show.

80
00:06:05,460 --> 00:06:12,600
It bounces instructions off a satellite and down to the electrical controls tied to each batch of charges.

81
00:06:14,380 --> 00:06:19,420
We will be sending signals out to the fireworks on the barges as well as on the bridge.

82
00:06:19,760 --> 00:06:24,180
We're all tied in to the central time clock coming off the satellites.

83
00:06:24,181 --> 00:06:29,520
they can run independently but the backup system is all coming through here to keep them all interlinked

84
00:06:31,860 --> 00:06:38,760
throughout the afternoon and evening the crowd gathers and anticipation grows the main event is

85
00:06:38,761 --> 00:06:45,540
preceded by an elaborate airshow featuring everything from war reenactments to UFOs

86
00:06:45,541 --> 00:06:57,300
as the clock ticks down director Tim Creed is the one man with his finger on the abort button any

87
00:06:57,301 --> 00:07:04,140
sign of malfunction or safety breach can put the show on hold and disappoint an eager audience that

88
00:07:04,141 --> 00:07:11,400
includes millions of television viewers nobody wants a delay the time when everybody gets a

89
00:07:11,401 --> 00:07:16,680
chance to calm down is when the fireworks go. When the fireworks start, it's all on

90
00:07:16,681 --> 00:07:21,240
computer and we really get to sit back and enjoy it. It's a lot of fun. At 930,

91
00:07:21,241 --> 00:07:25,880
it's a different situation than 925.

92
00:07:26,480 --> 00:07:33,720
Precisely at 930 p.m., the sky over Louisville ignites.

93
00:07:41,400 --> 00:07:53,040
One of the early effects is the dazzling Zembele rainbow.

94
00:07:53,820 --> 00:08:00,480
Every color that fireworks technology is currently capable of creating appears above the 2nd Street Bridge.

95
00:08:00,481 --> 00:08:09,800
Special iridescent violet, cyan and tangerine shades developed in Spain complete the 1.5 mile rainbow.

96
00:08:11,400 --> 00:08:24,860
Sticks of aluminum powder running the length of the bridge provide a cascading waterfall,

97
00:08:25,440 --> 00:08:28,300
longer than any natural one on Earth.

98
00:08:28,301 --> 00:08:42,301
by 9 52 the sky has gone dark and a satiated crowd believes the show is over

99
00:08:42,660 --> 00:08:46,000
but the calm is a false finale

100
00:08:46,001 --> 00:08:51,780
pause before the grandest finale the world will see this year

101
00:08:51,781 --> 00:08:58,120
in the next two minutes 6 000 shells will explode over louisville

102
00:08:58,121 --> 00:09:12,121
The show that thrilled Louisville was state of the art, but the technology behind it had

103
00:09:13,040 --> 00:09:17,280
come directly from the ancient invention of gunpowder.

104
00:09:21,860 --> 00:09:26,820
We now return to fireworks on Modern Marvels.

105
00:09:28,120 --> 00:09:35,620
The origins of fireworks date back at least a thousand years and are traced to the Far East.

106
00:09:36,500 --> 00:09:39,880
Most historians agree that the Chinese invented black powder,

107
00:09:40,520 --> 00:09:44,640
the original gunpowder and the source of pyrotechnic explosions.

108
00:09:45,500 --> 00:09:51,240
Legend has it that a Chinese chemist accidentally combined saltpeter or potassium nitrate

109
00:09:51,241 --> 00:09:57,060
with two fire starters, sulfur and charcoal, and thus stumbled onto a combustible mixture.

110
00:09:58,120 --> 00:10:02,780
The Chinese made firecrackers out of this by confining it in tubes.

111
00:10:02,920 --> 00:10:07,060
They made rockets that shot up in the air and produced pyrotechnic effects

112
00:10:07,061 --> 00:10:10,680
and really created the beginnings of today's fireworks industry.

113
00:10:12,580 --> 00:10:16,920
Early fireworks burned in pale shades of white and gold,

114
00:10:17,640 --> 00:10:21,980
the natural tints of sulfur, charcoal, and potassium nitrate.

115
00:10:22,580 --> 00:10:27,100
When the powder was packed into closed bamboo tubes, the firecracker was born.

116
00:10:27,101 --> 00:10:34,100
A tube with one open end created the first skyrockets.

117
00:10:34,101 --> 00:10:43,100
The Chinese imbued the mysterious and powerful new force with religious meaning, using its

118
00:10:43,101 --> 00:10:46,100
sound to ward off evil spirits.

119
00:10:46,101 --> 00:10:53,100
In time, most significant events, births, weddings, funerals, were marked by the ritual

120
00:10:53,101 --> 00:10:56,100
display of fireworks.

121
00:10:56,101 --> 00:11:02,780
When Western explorers saw firecrackers and skyrockets,

122
00:11:02,940 --> 00:11:05,760
they immediately recognized the military potential.

123
00:11:06,660 --> 00:11:08,520
They brought gunpowder back to Europe,

124
00:11:08,521 --> 00:11:12,700
where the English monk Roger Bacon cracked the mystery of its composition.

125
00:11:14,580 --> 00:11:19,580
Bacon feared the invention and recorded his formula in a secret code.

126
00:11:21,060 --> 00:11:23,760
But the Pandora's box was open,

127
00:11:23,761 --> 00:11:30,160
And by the 1400s, gunpowder factories had sprouted up from England to Italy.

128
00:11:31,160 --> 00:11:37,100
European nations quickly learned that the new technology could shift the balance of power.

129
00:11:38,020 --> 00:11:45,060
The real important person in 15th, 16th century military technology was the powder maker.

130
00:11:45,480 --> 00:11:50,560
Because someone who could produce and knew all the secrets to make a good black powder, a good gunpowder,

131
00:11:50,561 --> 00:11:53,060
determined how far your cannons could fire.

132
00:11:53,061 --> 00:12:00,380
And of course in naval warfare, as soon as your ship gets within firing range of the enemy's ship, you open fire.

133
00:12:00,780 --> 00:12:06,480
And if you have an excellent black powder, it would give you an enhanced firing range and military superiority.

134
00:12:08,740 --> 00:12:11,920
Gunpowder and fireworks continued to spread.

135
00:12:13,180 --> 00:12:20,980
Marco Polo imported the formula to Italy, making his homeland the fireworks capital of the world for centuries to come.

136
00:12:23,060 --> 00:12:32,820
The Italians crafted fireworks fountains, an effect created when iron filings were added to the mix and sprayed out in a stream of fire.

137
00:12:38,040 --> 00:12:41,780
Royalty across Europe took full advantage of their new toy.

138
00:12:42,680 --> 00:12:48,200
In Russia, Peter the Great loved noise and delighted in personally setting off charges.

139
00:12:48,201 --> 00:12:57,800
When his son Alexis was born in 1690, Peter assaulted the ears of Moscow residents with a deafening five-hour salute.

140
00:13:02,300 --> 00:13:06,880
Military victories were invariably celebrated by showers of fire.

141
00:13:07,900 --> 00:13:15,680
In 1749, the end of the Austrian War of Succession was marked by a memorable display of nationalistic pride.

142
00:13:18,200 --> 00:13:24,200
There were celebrations all over Europe, and in Paris they had one where the Italians and the French,

143
00:13:24,201 --> 00:13:28,200
pyrotechnicians got into a big argument about who was going to set off the show.

144
00:13:28,201 --> 00:13:31,079
The fight broke out, the fireworks went off.

145
00:13:31,080 --> 00:13:35,200
By mistake, 300 people were wounded, some 40 or 50 were killed.

146
00:13:35,201 --> 00:13:38,479
All this for the honor of setting off the fireworks.

147
00:13:38,480 --> 00:13:42,200
French and Italians, great tempers, and everything went up.

148
00:13:42,201 --> 00:13:43,200
Boom.

149
00:13:43,201 --> 00:13:52,360
Extravagant structures known as temples or machines provided a majestic backdrop for

150
00:13:52,361 --> 00:13:54,360
displays.

151
00:13:54,361 --> 00:14:00,600
These facades could reach hundreds of feet into the sky and acted as grand, albeit temporary,

152
00:14:00,601 --> 00:14:02,600
launching pads.

153
00:14:02,601 --> 00:14:10,300
A fireworks show would often involve building an elaborate set, a frame, and then they would

154
00:14:10,301 --> 00:14:13,920
They would put fountains, they would put wheels that would rise into the air.

155
00:14:14,200 --> 00:14:18,420
They would fill this framework with a number of different types of fireworks devices.

156
00:14:18,960 --> 00:14:21,840
It was frequently the case that the set would catch fire

157
00:14:21,841 --> 00:14:28,020
and the grand finale would be the burning of the entire frame as the end of the evening show.

158
00:14:30,700 --> 00:14:36,140
Early in the 1800s, fireworks displays began to employ innovative set pieces.

159
00:14:36,141 --> 00:14:41,640
A wooden framework was built in the shape of a face or more complex scene.

160
00:14:42,800 --> 00:14:46,520
Approximately every four inches a charge was attached to the frame,

161
00:14:46,880 --> 00:14:49,340
and a fuse ran the length of the contraption.

162
00:14:51,220 --> 00:14:56,620
When lit, a fiery image appeared, as in this portrait of President Herbert Hoover.

163
00:14:56,621 --> 00:15:08,060
The largest set piece ever built was in England at the Crystal Palace display in 1898.

164
00:15:09,280 --> 00:15:16,320
A structure hundreds of feet long depicted a massive collage of quaint houses and striking buildings.

165
00:15:18,480 --> 00:15:25,440
From its Chinese origins up until 1800, fireworks presentation had advanced,

166
00:15:25,441 --> 00:15:29,220
while the underlying science remained largely unchanged.

167
00:15:30,020 --> 00:15:33,280
But with the industrial age came modern chemistry

168
00:15:33,281 --> 00:15:35,800
and vividly colored minerals.

169
00:15:36,680 --> 00:15:38,440
Strontium compounds burned red

170
00:15:38,441 --> 00:15:41,800
while copper gave off a blue-green flame.

171
00:15:44,200 --> 00:15:47,060
Americans flocked to the exciting new colors,

172
00:15:47,580 --> 00:15:49,860
producing a boom in consumer fireworks.

173
00:15:51,020 --> 00:15:53,940
Mass market ads directed primarily at children

174
00:15:53,941 --> 00:15:57,520
became a staple of American magazines and catalogs.

175
00:15:58,680 --> 00:16:03,100
Colorful cartoon-like images enticed the young to join in on the fun.

176
00:16:09,520 --> 00:16:12,280
This age of innocence ended abruptly

177
00:16:12,281 --> 00:16:15,660
when the United States joined the First World War.

178
00:16:17,980 --> 00:16:20,500
The skies went dark on the 4th of July

179
00:16:20,501 --> 00:16:22,080
during the First and Second World Wars,

180
00:16:22,081 --> 00:16:25,452
and the fireworks companies and manufacturers in the U.S.

181
00:16:25,453 --> 00:16:27,760
were converted into ordnance producers.

182
00:16:28,200 --> 00:16:30,600
They were a valuable resource for the country to have

183
00:16:30,601 --> 00:16:36,440
in that they provided an experienced group of powder mixers and blenders

184
00:16:36,441 --> 00:16:38,560
and people who were used to handling explosives.

185
00:16:38,780 --> 00:16:40,280
They greatly aided our war effort.

186
00:16:42,020 --> 00:16:45,180
Fireworks had traveled the length and breadth of the globe,

187
00:16:45,980 --> 00:16:49,660
migrating from China to Europe to the United States.

188
00:16:49,661 --> 00:16:55,380
the business had transformed itself to help safeguard the freedoms of the new world.

189
00:16:56,340 --> 00:17:00,500
In the years to come, a determination to celebrate that same freedom

190
00:17:00,501 --> 00:17:03,900
would bring fireworks to new heights of brilliance.

191
00:17:07,060 --> 00:17:11,480
The development of fireworks was hastened by ancient alchemists

192
00:17:11,481 --> 00:17:15,820
who were attempting to convert common minerals into silver and gold.

193
00:17:15,821 --> 00:17:20,160
Fireworks will continue in a moment on Modern Markets.

194
00:17:21,660 --> 00:17:26,420
We now return to Fireworks on Modern Markets.

195
00:17:29,660 --> 00:17:36,420
In the United States, the custom of marking Independence Day with fireworks began in 1777,

196
00:17:36,900 --> 00:17:39,740
six years before the Revolutionary War ended.

197
00:17:40,660 --> 00:17:43,000
John Adams had asked his compatriots

198
00:17:43,001 --> 00:17:46,820
to solemnize their freedom with bonfires and illuminations

199
00:17:46,821 --> 00:17:49,500
from one end of this continent to the other,

200
00:17:49,860 --> 00:17:52,160
and they responded eagerly.

201
00:17:56,420 --> 00:17:58,520
By the late 19th century,

202
00:17:58,740 --> 00:18:01,500
fireworks had become affordable for the common man,

203
00:18:02,200 --> 00:18:04,920
and private citizens from New York to California

204
00:18:04,921 --> 00:18:07,080
staged jarring celebrations.

205
00:18:09,740 --> 00:18:18,280
A backyard fireworks show in this country, say at 1900, would have been filled with large explosive devices.

206
00:18:18,540 --> 00:18:20,005
There were no regulations.

207
00:18:20,006 --> 00:18:24,400
You could go out and buy large quarter sticks of dynamite to make a loud bang,

208
00:18:24,620 --> 00:18:27,820
let everybody in the town know that you were celebrating the Fourth of July.

209
00:18:29,280 --> 00:18:33,440
From a young boy's point of view, the Fourth of July was fireworks paradise.

210
00:18:33,960 --> 00:18:36,740
The availability of fireworks was widespread.

211
00:18:36,741 --> 00:18:39,740
he could walk into the local dime store,

212
00:18:39,741 --> 00:18:43,740
buy whatever his meager pocketbook would afford,

213
00:18:43,741 --> 00:18:46,740
and have the time of his life.

214
00:18:46,741 --> 00:18:48,740
By early in the 20th century,

215
00:18:48,741 --> 00:18:52,740
the 4th of July had become a major national event,

216
00:18:52,741 --> 00:18:55,740
more important than before or since.

217
00:18:55,741 --> 00:18:59,740
While most children delighted in their power to make noise,

218
00:18:59,741 --> 00:19:05,740
each year a few unlucky revelers were maimed or killed in explosions.

219
00:19:05,741 --> 00:19:13,860
Blank cartridge pistols were popular, despite their tendency to shatter and embed pieces of metal in young hands.

220
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:22,940
What often happened was that a child would have an accident like this,

221
00:19:23,060 --> 00:19:28,260
which by today's standards we would consider relatively mild in most cases,

222
00:19:28,840 --> 00:19:35,500
but back during those days, the ensuing tetanus infection took a lot of lives.

223
00:19:35,740 --> 00:19:42,720
Over the years, approximately 4,000 Americans died celebrating their independence,

224
00:19:43,120 --> 00:19:48,820
more than the number of American soldiers who perished in battle during the Revolutionary War.

225
00:19:49,760 --> 00:19:56,440
The same social forces pressing for prohibition of alcohol embarked on an anti-fireworks crusade.

226
00:19:57,540 --> 00:20:00,220
Calls rose for a safer and saner fourth.

227
00:20:00,221 --> 00:20:10,040
Between 1910 and 1914, several states passed laws restricting the sales and distribution of certain types of fireworks.

228
00:20:13,200 --> 00:20:22,220
While new laws regulated the most dangerous consumer explosives, fireworks continued to be marketed to children well into the 1940s.

229
00:20:22,221 --> 00:20:32,220
Elaborate catalogs enticed them with low-tech action figures who often ended their brief existence in a glorious burst of self-destruction.

230
00:20:32,221 --> 00:20:40,220
There was an SOS fire engine and it would let off a terrible siren and then blow up, which is very unlikely for a fire engine to do.

231
00:20:40,221 --> 00:20:42,158
There was even an SOS outhouse.

232
00:20:42,159 --> 00:20:48,220
You would see the smoke coming out of the half moon and a shriek and then bang, it would blow up.

233
00:20:48,221 --> 00:20:51,220
It was a marvelous series of things you could find in those catalogs.

234
00:20:53,220 --> 00:20:56,220
As consumer fireworks became more limited,

235
00:20:56,221 --> 00:21:01,220
towns and cities developed the custom of grand communal celebrations.

236
00:21:01,221 --> 00:21:04,220
One of the great rituals of small-town America

237
00:21:04,221 --> 00:21:08,220
was young and old gathering in the town square

238
00:21:08,221 --> 00:21:11,220
for the annual Independence Day bash.

239
00:21:13,220 --> 00:21:15,220
It was an occasion, and you learn for the first time

240
00:21:15,221 --> 00:21:17,220
that there was pleasure in a community gathering.

241
00:21:17,221 --> 00:21:19,300
community gathering because you were all there to see something.

242
00:21:19,720 --> 00:21:22,840
Except it was better than the theater, it was better than the opera, it was better than the concert because

243
00:21:22,841 --> 00:21:23,541
it was surprising.

244
00:21:23,542 --> 00:21:26,660
You never knew what was going to happen quite, what you were going to see in the sky.

245
00:21:27,720 --> 00:21:31,080
For young children, the fireworks man was a figure

246
00:21:31,081 --> 00:21:35,160
of great mystery and respect. He held the power

247
00:21:35,161 --> 00:21:37,840
to unite and thrill the entire town.

248
00:21:38,900 --> 00:21:43,340
And, like life itself, the pace of fireworks shows

249
00:21:43,341 --> 00:21:44,900
was slower in those days.

250
00:21:44,901 --> 00:21:53,320
a firework show 50 years ago would typically last an hour each shell would be fired up in the air

251
00:21:53,321 --> 00:21:59,760
it would burst the crowd would who and ah 10 seconds later another shell would go up

252
00:21:59,761 --> 00:22:08,660
in 1925 1930 that was big entertainment many of those depression era shows were created by

253
00:22:08,661 --> 00:22:11,132
relatively recent immigrant families.

254
00:22:11,133 --> 00:22:15,140
Ever since Marco Polo brought the formula for gunpowder from

255
00:22:15,141 --> 00:22:22,260
China to Italy, the Italians have been renowned fireworks craftsmen, searching for a better life.

256
00:22:22,261 --> 00:22:26,340
They came to America and brought with them their secret recipes.

257
00:22:28,740 --> 00:22:34,260
For centuries, the most closely guarded secrets in the fireworks industry were the formulas,

258
00:22:34,261 --> 00:22:40,220
the recipes to make the perfect gold color, the perfect green color, the perfect gold spark effect.

259
00:22:40,780 --> 00:22:46,160
Books with recipes and formulas were handed down from grandfather to father to son,

260
00:22:46,660 --> 00:22:48,180
regarded very, very closely.

261
00:22:48,420 --> 00:22:52,520
You would reveal the family finances before you'd reveal the family formulas.

262
00:22:54,460 --> 00:23:02,180
In 1870, pyrotechnician Angelo Lanzetta moved his family from Italy to Long Island, New York.

263
00:23:04,260 --> 00:23:09,440
By the 1920s, his great nephew, Felix Gruzzi, was head of the family.

264
00:23:10,940 --> 00:23:16,600
During the Depression, Felix and his brother made a brief foray to Miami in search of business.

265
00:23:18,700 --> 00:23:24,100
My dad and my uncle thought that the grass would be greener during the Depression time down in Miami.

266
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:28,060
Their first client happened to be a call from Al Capone.

267
00:23:28,700 --> 00:23:32,380
At first, my father was, gee, I don't know if we should do this. This is Al Capone.

268
00:23:32,381 --> 00:23:36,920
and he was afraid, but they were desperate for money, let's say,

269
00:23:36,960 --> 00:23:38,340
and it was their first customer,

270
00:23:38,920 --> 00:23:41,620
so he and my uncle decided to do the program.

271
00:23:43,440 --> 00:23:47,020
Local authorities denied Capone a permit for his show,

272
00:23:47,680 --> 00:23:51,360
forcing its cancellation and letting the Gruzis off the hook.

273
00:23:52,060 --> 00:23:55,440
The very next day, they awoke to screaming headlines,

274
00:23:56,040 --> 00:23:59,400
announcing the St. Valentine's Day massacre in Chicago.

275
00:23:59,401 --> 00:24:06,400
Capone, it seemed, had planned the fireworks party in Miami to provide himself with an alibi.

276
00:24:06,401 --> 00:24:10,400
The two of them looked at each other and said,

277
00:24:10,401 --> 00:24:13,400
oh my gosh, this is not something we want to be involved in.

278
00:24:13,401 --> 00:24:17,400
They kind of packed their bags up, left Miami, came back to New York,

279
00:24:17,401 --> 00:24:20,400
came out to Long Island, and that's where they've been ever since.

280
00:24:23,400 --> 00:24:26,400
For Felix Grucci and his family, fireworks was a calling.

281
00:24:26,401 --> 00:24:27,862
fireworks was a calling.

282
00:24:27,863 --> 00:24:31,640
They worked from dawn until after dark, perfecting their craft

283
00:24:31,641 --> 00:24:34,580
and building an enduring business.

284
00:24:34,581 --> 00:24:39,791
When we were small and young kids, we never went on vacations.

285
00:24:39,792 --> 00:24:41,640
Our vacations would be

286
00:24:41,641 --> 00:24:45,999
going to a firework program, wherever it may be, with my mother and father.

287
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:46,700
And it was

288
00:24:46,701 --> 00:24:48,160
their way of life.

289
00:24:48,161 --> 00:24:52,900
The Grucci's, of course, from the patriarch, old Felix, down to the very youngest, have

290
00:24:52,901 --> 00:24:55,200
I've always had this great passion about fireworks.

291
00:24:55,320 --> 00:24:56,680
The one I particularly remember was Jimmy,

292
00:24:57,140 --> 00:24:59,280
who had a system set up in his house

293
00:24:59,281 --> 00:25:01,700
so that when the alarm clock would go off in the morning,

294
00:25:01,840 --> 00:25:05,640
on would come the Tchaikovsky's 1812 overture in the middle of it

295
00:25:05,641 --> 00:25:07,000
when there's a huge firework burst,

296
00:25:07,080 --> 00:25:09,920
so the whole house would reverberate in fireworks and Tchaikovsky.

297
00:25:11,060 --> 00:25:14,500
By the 1970s, the Grucci's had established themselves

298
00:25:14,501 --> 00:25:18,360
as one of the premier fireworks companies in the United States.

299
00:25:18,361 --> 00:25:24,300
Felix, the patriarch, had accomplished much

300
00:25:24,301 --> 00:25:27,900
But his children urged him to raise his sights even higher

301
00:25:27,901 --> 00:25:33,620
They wanted the family to enter the world's most prestigious fireworks competition

302
00:25:33,621 --> 00:25:35,520
In Monte Carlo

303
00:25:35,521 --> 00:25:39,660
It took a long time between my mother and myself

304
00:25:39,661 --> 00:25:40,980
But we got them convinced

305
00:25:40,981 --> 00:25:42,740
Okay, we're going to do this

306
00:25:42,741 --> 00:25:45,180
But dad, we're going to go over there and we're going to win

307
00:25:45,181 --> 00:25:46,540
I guarantee you we're going to win

308
00:25:46,541 --> 00:25:47,960
And what if we don't win?

309
00:25:47,961 --> 00:25:49,520
we're going to be disgraced.

310
00:25:49,660 --> 00:25:52,120
You know, it was a very big thing with Italian fathers

311
00:25:52,121 --> 00:25:55,540
in small businesses anyway, the word disgrace.

312
00:25:57,460 --> 00:26:01,240
In 1979, the Grucci's trek to Monte Carlo

313
00:26:01,241 --> 00:26:02,840
to take on the world.

314
00:26:03,600 --> 00:26:05,560
From the beginning, their effort was dogged

315
00:26:05,561 --> 00:26:07,180
by logistical nightmares.

316
00:26:08,280 --> 00:26:10,400
They brought thousands of shells,

317
00:26:10,820 --> 00:26:13,920
none of which fit the local metric-sized mortars.

318
00:26:13,921 --> 00:26:22,020
Quickly adapting, they bought every container of Vaseline they could find and used it to lubricate the mortars.

319
00:26:22,700 --> 00:26:29,540
Master fireworks maker, Jimmy Groci from Belport, Long Island, United States of America.

320
00:26:29,960 --> 00:26:34,100
The entire show had been synchronized to an intricate electrical system.

321
00:26:34,720 --> 00:26:38,340
When some of the fuses failed, the shells had to be fired manually,

322
00:26:39,060 --> 00:26:42,660
destroying the precise timing that had been planned for months.

323
00:26:42,661 --> 00:26:44,820
Okay, Dick, go start shooting.

324
00:26:45,660 --> 00:26:50,440
All the problems were forgotten when the Grucci's unleashed their grand finale.

325
00:27:00,860 --> 00:27:04,960
Two minutes of thunderous noise shook Monte Carlo.

326
00:27:05,920 --> 00:27:10,220
Gamblers ran out from the casinos to see if they were under attack.

327
00:27:12,660 --> 00:27:16,600
The next day, the competition judge announced the winner.

328
00:27:16,601 --> 00:27:22,460
...that Spain has received the second prize

329
00:27:22,461 --> 00:27:28,620
and that Mr. Gucci of the United States has been awarded the first prize.

330
00:27:30,080 --> 00:27:33,880
I had never seen my dad cry. He actually started to cry.

331
00:27:34,460 --> 00:27:36,980
It still makes me very emotional to see.

332
00:27:37,460 --> 00:27:40,280
I mean, he was in his 70s when this had happened,

333
00:27:40,281 --> 00:27:45,800
and it was such a proud moment for him, and I saw my brother embrace him, and it was just great.

334
00:27:45,960 --> 00:27:48,360
We will never forget that as long as we live, none of us.

335
00:27:48,460 --> 00:27:51,300
It was a very exciting, proud moment for us.

336
00:27:53,700 --> 00:27:59,900
The victory at Monte Carlo pushed both the Grucci's and American fireworks to the forefront.

337
00:28:00,900 --> 00:28:05,860
A century after coming to America, the Grucci's had surpassed their old friends back in Italy.

338
00:28:05,861 --> 00:28:11,020
Just a few years later, they would team with other immigrant families

339
00:28:11,021 --> 00:28:14,140
to create a memorable tribute to their ancestors

340
00:28:14,141 --> 00:28:17,300
and to the land that had welcomed them.

341
00:28:22,540 --> 00:28:27,260
We now return to fireworks on Modern Models.

342
00:28:27,261 --> 00:28:38,760
in the years following their triumph at the monte carlo festival the gucci family continued to fine

343
00:28:38,761 --> 00:28:45,600
tune their technique each working day at their factory on long island ended with a lively test

344
00:28:45,601 --> 00:28:51,820
of the latest shells our neighbors used to enjoy the fireworks they used to come out every night

345
00:28:51,821 --> 00:28:56,860
they knew when it was five o'clock because the five o'clock would be the blast they used to watch

346
00:28:56,861 --> 00:28:58,460
It was like a fireworks show to them.

347
00:29:00,820 --> 00:29:03,820
One of the Grucci's most memorable accomplishments

348
00:29:03,821 --> 00:29:08,800
was the centennial commemoration of the Brooklyn Bridge in 1983.

349
00:29:09,920 --> 00:29:14,100
A century earlier, the bridge had opened to a glorious fireworks display

350
00:29:14,101 --> 00:29:15,880
captured by vivid paintings.

351
00:29:16,920 --> 00:29:19,640
The Grucci's were determined to surpass the original.

352
00:29:26,860 --> 00:29:32,700
Traffic on the bridge was slowed and later stopped as the Gucci team planted its charges.

353
00:29:39,580 --> 00:29:45,340
Then just three hours before showtime, a fireboat cruised underneath the bridge

354
00:29:45,341 --> 00:29:47,820
and fired its water hoses into the air.

355
00:29:51,260 --> 00:29:54,860
We were like, oh my god, this boat's gonna wet everything.

356
00:29:54,861 --> 00:29:57,000
If it gets wet, it's not going to go off.

357
00:29:57,320 --> 00:30:00,640
My mother, she goes on every program, she wears her rosary beads around her neck.

358
00:30:00,740 --> 00:30:02,800
Boy, she started praying real quick at that point.

359
00:30:03,320 --> 00:30:06,800
She must have went through those beads like there was no tomorrow on it.

360
00:30:08,840 --> 00:30:14,800
Fortunately, only a few charges were ruined, and the show went off in spectacular fashion.

361
00:30:24,860 --> 00:30:30,760
New Yorkers roared their approval.

362
00:30:30,761 --> 00:30:33,640
For many, the highlight was a Gucci invention.

363
00:30:34,640 --> 00:30:40,820
The bridge and river below meeting in a cascade of fire they dubbed Niagara Falls.

364
00:30:54,860 --> 00:31:05,320
The Grucci's were but one of the families from Italy that rose to prominence in American fireworks.

365
00:31:06,320 --> 00:31:13,220
The Zambelli's masterminds behind Thunder Over Louisville began their American journey in 1893.

366
00:31:14,400 --> 00:31:21,660
Antonio Zambelli settled in New Castle, Pennsylvania because its climate was similar to the region near Naples from which he hailed.

367
00:31:22,660 --> 00:31:26,860
When Antonio passed through Ellis Island en route to his new home,

368
00:31:27,260 --> 00:31:29,340
he carried his most precious possession,

369
00:31:30,100 --> 00:31:33,780
a little black book with the family fireworks recipes.

370
00:31:36,480 --> 00:31:39,940
They're still written in Italian, and we have them in a safe that's locked up,

371
00:31:39,941 --> 00:31:43,040
and really I'm going to have to teach one of my children how to get to them.

372
00:31:43,560 --> 00:31:46,760
And whenever I need any help, I have an Italian friend that reads them for me,

373
00:31:46,840 --> 00:31:50,740
and we translate them, and we're using those same formulas today.

374
00:31:51,660 --> 00:31:56,420
From the early years all the way into the 1960s,

375
00:31:57,020 --> 00:32:00,620
much of the Zambelli's business came from Italian Saints days.

376
00:32:02,620 --> 00:32:05,500
People would march behind the band down to the fireworks site,

377
00:32:05,840 --> 00:32:08,220
and that's how they got the people down to the site

378
00:32:08,221 --> 00:32:11,080
because it wasn't always right next to where the festival was being held.

379
00:32:11,460 --> 00:32:15,360
It was down a few blocks, and the band would play the Italian march

380
00:32:15,361 --> 00:32:17,760
all the way down to the field, and then the fireworks would go off.

381
00:32:17,761 --> 00:32:27,080
Each generation of pyrotechnicians has passed down its knowledge to its children and grandchildren.

382
00:32:27,860 --> 00:32:33,200
Until very recently, there were no formal studies of the chemistry involved in fireworks.

383
00:32:33,940 --> 00:32:37,080
You learn by doing, starting as early as possible.

384
00:32:40,080 --> 00:32:44,760
When I was 12, I actually got to get out and fire the shows for the first time

385
00:32:44,761 --> 00:32:46,940
and torch off a big 12-inch shell.

386
00:32:46,941 --> 00:32:55,779
So I got to get involved at a very young age, where now today, with all the licensing procedures, particularly here in California, you're not even allowed to be on

387
00:32:55,780 --> 00:32:57,460
site until you're 18 years old.

388
00:32:58,380 --> 00:33:06,660
Jim Souza of Pyro Spectaculars runs yet another immigrant family dynasty, the Souzas of California.

389
00:33:07,560 --> 00:33:14,000
Of Portuguese heritage, the Souzas have been displaying their unique brand of showmanship for nearly a century.

390
00:33:15,000 --> 00:33:19,340
In recent decades, they have pioneered safer forms of fireworks.

391
00:33:20,540 --> 00:33:27,700
In 1969, my father and one of his co-workers were seriously injured in a display that they were firing off in San Francisco.

392
00:33:28,100 --> 00:33:32,800
And it occurred because of a six-inch shell detonated in the mortar and got my father.

393
00:33:32,980 --> 00:33:34,420
He'd lost the use of his hands.

394
00:33:34,421 --> 00:33:45,100
And from that moment after that accident, the whole family said we were going to do whatever we could to pioneer the firing of fireworks electronically.

395
00:33:48,120 --> 00:33:54,140
Since the beginning of fireworks, blasts had been triggered by manually igniting each shell.

396
00:33:55,260 --> 00:34:02,360
Electronic firing held the promise of putting distance and a measure of safety between technician and explosion.

397
00:34:02,361 --> 00:34:05,760
and by permitting central control of the shells,

398
00:34:05,960 --> 00:34:09,800
electronic ignition also opened up an entirely new creative vista.

399
00:34:10,520 --> 00:34:14,940
For the first time, hundreds and thousands of blasts could be planned

400
00:34:14,941 --> 00:34:17,220
and synchronized to the second.

401
00:34:25,740 --> 00:34:31,660
By the 1980s, electronic firing had become the standard for major programs.

402
00:34:32,360 --> 00:34:38,160
the new technology would be put to its most extensive use at one of the largest displays ever attempted,

403
00:34:39,060 --> 00:34:42,340
the 1986 centennial of the Statue of Liberty.

404
00:34:43,120 --> 00:34:49,600
For this once-in-a-lifetime event, three proud and competitive families join forces.

405
00:34:50,600 --> 00:34:53,840
It was too much for one fireworks company to do,

406
00:34:54,280 --> 00:35:00,000
so the three big fireworks companies in this country, the Zambellis, the Souzas, and the Grucci's,

407
00:35:00,001 --> 00:35:02,540
came together to work on one project.

408
00:35:02,920 --> 00:35:04,480
People said, this will never work.

409
00:35:04,600 --> 00:35:05,720
This can't be done.

410
00:35:05,960 --> 00:35:07,700
But they came together, they cooperated,

411
00:35:07,820 --> 00:35:10,240
they joined forces and put on one of the most spectacular

412
00:35:10,241 --> 00:35:12,460
fireworks shows this country has ever seen

413
00:35:12,461 --> 00:35:14,900
and probably will see for a number of years to come.

414
00:35:17,680 --> 00:35:20,120
For the Statue of Liberty celebration,

415
00:35:20,620 --> 00:35:22,600
the three families fired shells

416
00:35:22,601 --> 00:35:26,580
from 33 different launch sites across New York Harbor.

417
00:35:27,400 --> 00:35:29,320
Modern Marvels will return.

418
00:35:30,000 --> 00:35:35,260
We now return to fireworks on Modern Martins.

419
00:35:40,540 --> 00:35:49,900
No single event in history has done more to expand the popularity of fireworks than the United States bicentennial in 1976.

420
00:35:50,700 --> 00:35:58,560
Dazzled by shows in New York Harbor and around the country, consumers began to see fireworks as its own form of entertainment.

421
00:35:58,561 --> 00:36:02,560
Rock concerts and sporting events soon incorporated the craze.

422
00:36:19,560 --> 00:36:22,560
Amateur pyrotechnicians staged a resurgence.

423
00:36:22,561 --> 00:36:29,560
pyrotechnics guild international or pgi was formed by serious hobbyists as a forum to share their eccentric passion

424
00:36:40,560 --> 00:36:44,560
their annual convention concludes with the lighting of a super string

425
00:36:44,561 --> 00:36:49,560
a lengthy series of explosives worthy of any professional company

426
00:37:14,660 --> 00:37:15,860
They're great fun.

427
00:37:16,060 --> 00:37:18,180
They're hugely enthusiastic people.

428
00:37:18,181 --> 00:37:22,120
I went to one where there was a moment of silence when one of their members had died.

429
00:37:22,560 --> 00:37:27,260
And his ashes were sent up in this absolutely spectacular chrysanthemum shell.

430
00:37:27,940 --> 00:37:31,360
And somebody said, this is a memory of Bill Thomasville.

431
00:37:31,620 --> 00:37:32,980
We all watched this lovely shell.

432
00:37:33,060 --> 00:37:35,580
May have made it himself for all I know in this great best.

433
00:37:35,660 --> 00:37:37,660
We all remembered him and remembered the shells he made.

434
00:37:37,740 --> 00:37:38,780
It's all very beautiful stuff.

435
00:37:39,840 --> 00:37:44,500
The increased worldwide demand has triggered a jump in fireworks' quality.

436
00:37:45,860 --> 00:37:48,520
Colors are more vivid now than 20 years ago.

437
00:37:48,521 --> 00:37:53,520
Electronic firing has brought precision to performances,

438
00:37:53,521 --> 00:37:58,520
and modern science is finally catching up with an ancient art.

439
00:37:58,521 --> 00:38:02,520
Fireworks chemistry is a very complex area of chemistry.

440
00:38:02,521 --> 00:38:06,520
You're dealing with very, very fast chemical reactions, explosions.

441
00:38:06,521 --> 00:38:09,520
You're dealing with the disappearance of your reaction products.

442
00:38:09,521 --> 00:38:12,520
There's nothing left to analyze when the burst is over.

443
00:38:12,521 --> 00:38:19,640
So fireworks technology for centuries was much more an art than it was a science.

444
00:38:20,480 --> 00:38:25,760
Chemistry has allowed pyrotechnicians to understand how their materials work.

445
00:38:27,220 --> 00:38:30,540
Computers have given them unprecedented creative freedom.

446
00:38:31,640 --> 00:38:35,300
A show designer can now chart out a program burst by burst,

447
00:38:36,380 --> 00:38:38,980
changing the sequence with the touch of a button.

448
00:38:38,981 --> 00:38:45,900
in recent years so-called specialty fireworks have expanded their repertoire

449
00:38:45,901 --> 00:38:54,440
at Universal Studios Hollywood this pyrotechnic show heralds the opening of the T2 3D attraction

450
00:38:54,441 --> 00:39:03,660
theatrical fireworks experts produce a wall of fire that nearly touches performers yet remains safe

451
00:39:03,661 --> 00:39:10,220
Close proximity fireworks contain powders that fire at a lower than normal temperature

452
00:39:10,221 --> 00:39:15,400
and consist of extremely small pellets that ensure a quick and safe burn.

453
00:39:16,200 --> 00:39:20,220
And in a break with tradition, their contents are mixed by machine.

454
00:39:23,520 --> 00:39:25,100
This is a look to the future.

455
00:39:25,680 --> 00:39:29,460
In most firework applications, the manufacturing is done by hand.

456
00:39:29,461 --> 00:39:32,640
We've developed a state-of-the-art technology, this piece of machinery

457
00:39:32,641 --> 00:39:36,540
that allows us to build very special and very precise mini-gerbs.

458
00:39:36,700 --> 00:39:39,420
By the use of the computer and electronic scales,

459
00:39:39,600 --> 00:39:42,660
we can control the exact measurement of the powders

460
00:39:42,661 --> 00:39:45,360
so that we can control the burn time and the altitude

461
00:39:45,361 --> 00:39:48,360
for our mini-gerbs to be fired very safely on stage.

462
00:39:53,000 --> 00:39:56,280
When the Seusses were called on to provide entertainment

